    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Ca_tru -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Ca_tru">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Van_chuong-Tho_ca"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la xml:lang="en">Ca Tru (in Nom: 歌籌), also known as Hat Co Dau / Hat Nha Tro, is a type of performance using chamber music scales that is very popular in the Northern and North Central regions of Vietnam. Ca Tru has been popular since the 15th century, used to be a type of singing in the royal court and was loved by the nobility and intellectuals. Ca Tru is a smooth and perfect combination of poetry and music. Until the 1980s, this genre was often called Hat A Dao (literally &quot;hat xam cua dinh&quot;), however, in the 1990s, it was often called Hat Ca Tru. The word &quot;Ca Tru&quot; is said to be taken from Nom: 歌籌, meaning a way of singing with bamboo cards, the listener throws a card to the singer when they find a good part. Then they count the cards and pay in money. The singers are called a dao, however the word &quot;a&quot; is associated with prostitution, so it is called co dao, and the variant is co dau. Ca tru was once loved by the intellectual class in feudal times, performed in village temples and even in the royal court, then developed in guilds, using women and gradually changed. During the colonial period, ca tru developed in urban areas in singing houses where alcohol and opium were supplied. After 1945, when the Viet Minh came to power, it was criticized for being associated with prostitution activities of the old regime. After 1954, ca tru was officially banned, but was restored after the Doi Moi movement was launched and today is often considered a learned art form of traditional Vietnamese music.

On October 1, 2009, ca tru was listed as an intangible heritage in need of urgent protection. This is a UNESCO title in Vietnam with a large area of ​​influence, with a scope of 16 provinces and cities in the northern half of Vietnam. The nomination file for Ca Tru as a world cultural heritage with the Ca Tru cultural space spanning across 16 northern provinces including: Phu Tho, Vinh Phuc, Hanoi, Bac Giang, Bac Ninh, Hai Phong, Hai Duong, Hung Yen, Ha Nam, Nam Dinh, Thai Binh, Ninh Binh, Thanh Hoa, Nghe An, Ha Tinh and Quang Binh. Ca Tru is a masterpiece of oral and intangible heritage of humanity, second only to the Korean pansori ao dao.

On February 23, 2020, on the anniversary of the founder of Ca Tru, Google for the first time honored this traditional art form with a special temporary replacement icon on the Google homepage in Vietnam.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Cai_luong -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Cai_luong">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Co_truyen-Dan_gian"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la>Cai Luong (Chinese: 改良) is a type of theater originating from the South, formed on the basis of the Don ca tai tu music and folk songs of the Mekong Delta.

Explaining the word &quot;cai luong&quot; (改良) in Sino-Vietnamese, Professor Tran Van Khe said: &quot;Cai luong is to change to become better&quot;, expressed through the performance stage, script theme, performing art, orchestra and music. Here, it is the reformation (renovation, innovation) of the art of Hat Boi. From a verb in the common sense, it has become a proper noun. After Cai Luong, the art of Cai Luong is completely different from the art of Hat Boi in both content and form.

Regarding the time of its birth, according to Vuong Hong Sen: although &quot;some people think that Cai Luong had its beginnings in 1916, or 1918&quot;, according to him, since November 16, 1918, when the play Gia Long Tau Quoc was performed at the Tay Saigon Theater, this new way of singing &quot;continued to expand, opening a new profession, taking the music and singing to adjust, adding and adding, both innovating and reforming...so no one knows exactly when Cai Luong was formed...&quot;</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


