    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Cheo -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Cheo">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Co_truyen-Dan_gian"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la xml:lang="en">Cheo (Nom script: 掉) is a traditional Vietnamese theatrical art form. Cheo developed strongly in the north of Vietnam, focusing on the Red River Delta and two spreading areas: the midlands and mountains of the North and the North Central. This type of theater is highly developed and rich in national identity. Cheo has a popular character and is considered a type of festival theater with the characteristics of using polyphonic and polysemous language combined with metaphors rich in narrative and lyrical meaning. If traditional Chinese theater has a typical representative of Beijing Opera, then the most typical representative of traditional Vietnamese theater is Cheo. Cheo theater art has gone through a long historical process from the 10th century to the present, and has penetrated deeply into Vietnamese social life. Cheo fully reflects all aspects of Vietnamese national identity: optimistic, benevolent, loving a peaceful and simple life, but full of national pride, resilient in repelling foreign invaders, protecting the fatherland. Because of that healthy ideological content, Cheo has all the literary genres: lyrical, romantic, heroic, epic, didactic poetry... more than other art forms such as Tuong, Cai Luong, Ca Drama... From 2021, the art of Cheo in the Red River Delta has been submitted to UNESCO for recognition as a representative intangible cultural heritage of humanity.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


