    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Hat_ba_trao -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Hat_ba_trao">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Co_truyen-Dan_gian"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la xml:lang="en">Hat Ba Trao (also known as Cheo Ba Trao, Cheo Dua Linh, Ho Dua Linh, Ho Hau Linh) is a type of folk performing art with strong ritual characteristics of the residents of the Central coastal region, specifically from Binh Tri Thien (Quang Binh, Quang Tri, Thua Thien Hue) to Binh Thuan. The content and meaning of Ho Ba Trao is to pray for national peace and prosperity, favorable weather, calm seas, and good harvests for fishermen, expressing the unique cultural identity of the coastal region, the unity of mind and heart, mutual love of coastal fishermen, expressing the typical regional dialect and dialect. Besides, it shows the respect and gratitude of the coastal fishing community towards the Whale who has helped fishermen overcome storms and disasters at sea. At the same time, it also has a profound humanistic meaning, which is singing and rowing (the movement of holding the oar is different from the art of Northern rowing) to bring the souls, the unjust souls who died unexpectedly due to accidents, war, or sacrifice for the country.... to the place of rebirth in paradise.)&apos; Singing Ba Trao is a folk performing art recognized by the Socialist Republic of Vietnam as a national intangible cultural heritage, officially recognized on September 9, 2013.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Hat_chau_van -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Hat_chau_van">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Van_chuong-Tho_ca"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la xml:lang="en">Hat Van, also known as Chau Van, Hat Hau Dong, Hat Bong, is a traditional singing art form of Vietnam. This is a form of ritual music associated with the Hau Dong ritual of the Tu Phu (Mother Goddess) religion and the worship of Duc Thanh Tran (Duc Thanh Vuong Tran Hung Dao), a Vietnamese folk belief. By using spiritual music with solemn and refined lyrics, Chau Van is considered a form of singing with the meaning of Chau Thanh. Hat Van originated in the Northern Delta region.

The most prosperous period of Hat Van was in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. During this time, there were often singing competitions to select Cung Van singers. Since 1954, Hat Van has gradually faded away because Hau Dong was banned because it was considered superstitious. By the early 1990s, Hat Van had the opportunity to develop again. The centers of Hat Van are Nam Dinh, Ha Nam, Ninh Binh and some areas in the Northern Delta. The art of Chau Van is being preserved and has been recognized by UNESCO as an intangible cultural heritage. Currently, the Chau Van ritual of Nam Dinh people and the whole of Vietnam has been included in the list of 33 intangible cultural heritages of Vietnam (phase 1) and recognized as a World Heritage. In Hue, there is also a form of Chau Van, but the melody is very different from that of the Northern provinces.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Hat_xoan -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Hat_xoan">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Dan_ca"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la xml:lang="en">Xoan singing is a type of folk song, ritual and custom of singing to worship gods and tutelary gods with a multi-element art form: music, singing, dancing; often performed in early spring, popular in the land of Hung Vuong - Phu Tho, a province in the midland region of Vietnam. On November 24, 2011, at the 6th Conference of the Intergovernmental Committee for the Conservation of Intangible Cultural Heritage of UNESCO held in Bali - Indonesia, Vietnam&apos;s Xoan singing - Phu Tho dossier was recognized as an Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in need of urgent protection. On the evening of February 3, 2018, at Lai Len Temple, Kim Duc Commune, Viet Tri City, Phu Tho Province, Phu Tho Provincial People&apos;s Committee coordinated with the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism to organize a ceremony to receive the certificate of UNESCO recognizing Phu Tho Xoan singing as a representative intangible cultural heritage of humanity.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Hau_giang -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Hau_giang">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Dong_bang_song_cuu_long"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Ho -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Ho">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Dan_ca"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la>Ho (English: Chanty) is a popular musical performance genre in Vietnamese life since ancient times, originating from the living habits of lowland areas, expressing the mood of workers. Ho and ly are somewhat similar, but ho is often associated with a movement when working, while ly is not.

Some folk songs of other countries or regions with similar content and rhythm are also named &quot;ho&quot;, such as the Russian folk song Ho tugboat on the Volga River.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


