    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Nhac_cung_dinh -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Nhac_cung_dinh">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Co_truyen-Dan_gian"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la xml:lang="en">Hue Royal Court Music is a genre of royal court music in the feudal period, performed on festival occasions (coronation, death, other solemn festivals) during the year of the Nguyen Dynasty of Vietnam. Hue Royal Court Music was recognized by UNESCO as a Masterpiece of Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in 2003. According to UNESCO&apos;s assessment, &quot;among the traditional music genres in Vietnam, only Royal Court Music has reached national stature&quot;. &quot;Court music has been developed since the 13th century in Vietnam. During the Nguyen Dynasty, Hue Royal Court Music reached its most mature and complete level&quot;. Along with the Central Highlands Gong Cultural Space, this is an intangible heritage of Vietnam that has been officially recognized by UNESCO. Hue Royal Court Music is a legacy, since the orchestras - in which many court instruments were present - appeared in the form of reliefs on stone pillars of pagodas during the Ly Dynasty, in the 11th-12th centuries, until the last king of the Nguyen Dynasty abdicated in the mid-20th century.

Basically, ritual music in Vietnamese court music, sacrificial music in village temples as well as ritual music played at weddings or funerals, are all often divided into two main groups: the literary group and the martial group. The division of musical groups in the Hue royal court orchestra from the early 19th century and its origin have been found in the rules of many rituals of worshiping at communal houses of the Vietnamese people in the North from many centuries ago.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Nhac_tai_tu -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Nhac_tai_tu">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Co_truyen-Dan_gian"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la xml:lang="en">Southern amateur music is a Vietnamese folk music genre that was recognized by UNESCO as an intangible cultural heritage on December 5, 2013 and is a UNESCO title in Vietnam with a large area of ​​influence, covering 21 southern provinces and cities. Southern amateur music was formed and developed in the late 19th century, originating from ceremonial music, Hue royal court music and folk literature. Southern amateur music is a typical folk art form of the Southern region. This is an art form of instruments and songs, sung by ordinary people, young men and women in the Southern countryside after working hours. Southern amateur music appeared more than 100 years ago, as a performance with a band consisting of four instruments: the Kim (zither), the Co (zither), the Tranh (zither) and the Bau (called the Tu Tuyet). Later, there was innovation by replacing the Doc Huyen Cam with a guitar with concave frets. Most of the people who participate in amateur music are friends and neighbors. They gather to share their elegant hobby so they are not usually too particular about their attire.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Ninh_binh -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Ninh_binh">
