    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Vinh_phuc -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Vinh_phuc">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Dong_bang_song_hong"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Xam -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Xam">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Dan_ca"/>
        <duoc_mo_ta_la xml:lang="en">Xam is a type of Vietnamese folk song, popular in the plains and midlands of the North. &quot;Xam&quot; is also used to refer to people who practice Xam singing. Artist Ha Thi Cau (1928–2013) is considered the last Xam singer of the 20th century and Ninh Binh province is making efforts to submit to UNESCO to recognize Xam singing as a world cultural heritage in need of urgent protection.

Xam singing used to be a form of livelihood for the people, today Xam art is staged and brought to serve tourists. Xam is mostly performed in markets, streets, and crowded places. Xam singing is spontaneous and the performer can burst out singing right away while performing.</duoc_mo_ta_la>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Yen_bai -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Yen_bai">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/tranh/ontologies/2023/9/untitled-ontology-19#Tay_bac_bo"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


